  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\n- Rain ends, then clearing\n\nLocal radar shows light rain gradually ending from north to\nsouth early this morning as drier air begins to flow in behind a\ncold front that has just passed through the state. Overall, the\nrain was a good soaker and we won't see much in the way of\nprecipitation through next week. \n\nIR loop shows the back edge of the thick higher cloud deck moving\nacross northern Lake MI and mostly clear skies over Wisconsin. \nWe'll see clearing occur this morning leading to mostly sunny \nskies by this afternoon. \n\n- Frost tonight, Sunday night, Monday night \n\nA shot of cooler air behind the departing cold front, coupled with\nlight winds and clear skies tonight will enable good radiational\ncooling conditions. We're looking at a frost potential over the\nnorthern cwa and we've issued a frost advisory for several \ncounties of the northern two rows of counties, where temperatures \nwill fall into the lower to mid 30s. It's possible that a few low \nlying areas fall below freezing, but it's not clear it will be \nwidespread.\n\nAs the high drifts east through Saturday, southwest winds will\ndevelop and temperatures will climb from highs in the mid 60s\ntoday and Saturday to upper 60s Sunday. A secondary cold front\nwill move across the state Sunday afternoon and send h8\ntemperatures from 10c down to 0c by Monday morning. Frost is again\npossible over interior portions of the northern two rows of\ncounties. However, the coldest night will be Monday night.\nWidespread frost is possible across the entire cwa and sub\nfreezing temperatures over the northern row of counties in the\ncwa; a freeze warning may be needed there Monday night. \n\nTemperatures will warm into the upper 60s Tuesday through Friday\nnext week. Dewpoints in the 40s and 40s will make for comfortable\nfall conditions.",
